    "turn_idx": 50,
    "turn_text": "And so with the Senate passing such major legislation quickly and by the way, pretty bipartisan legislation and to program and the unemployment insurance, like the two hallmarks, unemployment insurance, as Chuck Schumer calls it, unemployment on steroids, was really the Democrat paycheck protection was designed to keep employers and employees connected. The Republican idea, the two came together and they came up with basically the first 100 or so pages of the 880 page CARES Act legislation. Remember, we passed it on a Monday in the paycheck for Friday, largely because there had to be a first day at some point that we should do that we had to get the money out the door to try to save as many employee situations. And the first day had its bumps and bruises. And then since that, it needed to evolve even after the first $350 billion went out. And by going out to many more low, smaller, smaller companies than larger, there's, you know, there've been some, some people might consider bad actors like the LA Lakers or franchises, but for them, it's hit the target that it was aimed at. And the rules, however, have been changing, or at least the guidance, maybe not so much rules as the guidance, and trying to aim at even more and more at smaller companies. North Dakota's done a great job. North Dakota, by the way, has the most loans per capita of any state in the country in their first tranche at 1,102. for a total of over one and a half",
