    "turn_text": "No question about it. Just yesterday I was watching CNBC and one of the major oil company CEOs who I know pretty well. He said, you know, what we need is more consolidation in the industry. And of course, I sort of grabbed gas, but he made my point for me, because what we don't need is more concentration. Now, free markets will create some of that. And sometimes that's appropriate. However, when it comes to a couple of things for sure, food and energy, especially when you're abundant in both, you ought to be able to, you know, energy independence ought to mean something. And that energy independence would be lost if we don't take care of our smaller producers, not big oil, but small to mid-size oil, which is largely what dominates North Dakota's landscape. And so a small and medium-sized companies have more debt than they have cash, highly leveraged. And of course, you can't, in North Dakota, you cannot make money on $10 oil, $20 oil, or even $30 oil. You literally can't And so what happens is if you concentrate all of that, which will be the logical outcome, if we don't provide, you know, some funding tools and some debt restructuring ability for smaller and mid-sized operators, what you're going to see is conical Philips and ExxonMobil and Philips 66, Chevron and maybe, you know, one or two others, Occidental, buy up these smaller private companies, in some case, smaller public companies. And then the capital investment in the future is going to go to where it gets the best return. And that's not going to be the Bakken. That's finally going to be West Texas. That's going to be Saudi Arabia. You know, Russia, which is why Saudi Arabia and Russia are trying to crush us out of business. And there are no quote bailouts for the oil and gas industry. We were able to get some expansion of the Main Street Lending Program. yesterday, which is, I think, you know, that create a lot of news today, national news, where I'm quoted a lot and I worked closely with Stephen Mnuchin, the Secretary of the Treasury and Secretary Bouillier, the Secretary of Energy and Jay Powell, the Chairman of the Federal Reserve Board to make sure that there's not a discrimination against oil and gas companies. And they expanded the Main Street program, which will help some of our companies. It's certainly not a silver boy. It's not free money. It's digital. It doesn't allow them to restructure debt, which I'll be working toward. I've been screaming at the large banks who have a strong bias against two industries, a strong bias against firearms and ammunition, and a strong bias against fossil And that the bias comes from pressure from the boards and their institutional investors who want to be the cool guys at the country club who say he comes, we don't need fossil fuels. We should only invest in clean green energy. Well, that's, that's bias against legal commerce.",
