    "turn_idx": 97,
    "turn_text": "I don't know that we're going to reveal anything new here today. But I think the issues with coal are really starting to come home to North Dakotans. I think that there are going to be some real wake up calls coming to people who have just sort of I don't know if they've been tuned in, but pretty soon we're at risk of losing probably 1,000, maybe 1,000s of jobs in Central North Dakota if we lose Cole Creek Station. Maybe that's the first domino to fall. Maybe some of these other stations go next, I don't know. And I think also the reality of what wind could mean on our grid, the investment that's gonna be required in transmission lines is gonna be enormous. That's gonna show up in our utility bills, that's gonna show up in our tax payments. And, you know, there's also the question of grid reliability. This is a very scary thing to me. And I say this not, obviously I like coal. I think coal's great, although I've always said that if we figure out a better way than coal than fine, you know, it should You know, horse and buggies were great until we invented the automobile, right? I'm not against, I'm not a Luddite. I'm not against technological progress. I'm not against the creative destruction of the free market, but that's not what this is. And I'm just, I'm feeling a little, I'm feeling trepidation. I think the coal plants have always played both in the economic impact that they've had, the jobs that they've provided, but also the reliable and cheap electricity they've provided in our region. have always been an advantage North Dakota has had, and I'm afraid that advantage is going away.",
